<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>France 24 - International breaking news, top stories and headlines</title><subtitle type="html">Breaking news and world news from France 24 on Business, Sports, Culture. Video news. News from the US, Europe, Asia Pacific, Africa, Middle East, America</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/rss</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:52Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>France: Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:eac9f6119ba85ecb1e29966fca5ebe70a1618410d3f0914ce5019edf4cb8a333</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:22Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T17:12:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off--ddfcca28f719.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off" /><summary type="html">Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest. Lecturer in Liberal Arts at Queen Mary University of London, Andrew Smith, shares further analysis.</summary></entry><entry><title>Opposition leader Machado returns to Venezuela after 10 months in exile</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:144366bb3e455f4938ce5ac7d562d56a72b89bd512dfa8216a6a5a298278c829</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:25Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T17:10:11Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/americas/20261025-opposition-leader-machado-returns-to-venezuela-after-10-months-in-exile--1872e0f5ffc4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261025-opposition-leader-machado-returns-to-venezuela-after-10-months-in-exile" /><summary type="html">Venezuelan opposition leader Maria Corina Machado announced on Sunday that she has returned to her homeland following almost a year in exile, after covertly leaving Venezuela in late 2025 to receive the Nobel Peace Prize. She returns as doubts about her country’s democratic future and the role of the US are growing.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>French far-right leader Le Pen kicks off presidential campaign</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c3e30810a2a770a9fe3e28c4fa508686a47a64e34adbcf618c9027a3fc2c0676</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T16:38:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/french-far-right-leader-le-pen-kicks-off-presidential-campaign--ab902b055a14.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-far-right-leader-le-pen-kicks-off-presidential-campaign" /><summary type="html">We’ve just been listening to Marine Le Pen, leader of the far-right National Rally and its presidential candidate, delivering her first official campaign speech in Orléans.

She spoke of what she called a “renaissance” for France and the dawn of a new era. She also condemned what she described as “Islamist ideologies” that promote violence, saying they are incompatible with France.

Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan was listening in Orléans and joins us now with more.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: 1.314 arrested during protests across the country</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:740f48b276fd4182d6c57f2325ce0a95f670ec31888bdd58e4f1f68da871abc0</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:31Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T16:32:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-1-314-arrested-during-protests-across-the-country--fc20118c2df1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-1-314-arrested-during-protests-across-the-country" /><summary type="html">France saw another day of nationwide protests on Saturday, with more than 400,000 people taking to the streets over the rising cost of living and school funding. Most marches remained peaceful, a week after riots and looting hit several cities. However, police say more than 1,300 people were arrested nationwide as violence broke out on the sidelines of demonstrations. Caroline Baum has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Midterms: Obama campaigns for Democrats, slams Trump</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:add359cf5de6c06aed2daa1dd8c99138b1db81a0fb40a778884daf6e66b669d2</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:34Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T16:25:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-midterms-obama-campaigns-for-democrats-slams-trump-1--61b9ef0e6d1e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-midterms-obama-campaigns-for-democrats-slams-trump-1" /><summary type="html">At a rally in Ohio this Saturday, former US president Barack Obama said that Americans were facing higher costs because of decisions made by the administration, including what he described as "an unnecessary war that nobody asked for and nobody voted for."</summary></entry><entry><title>French far-right leader Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e9d3d9ca6377270819e93e5cf79eadd2a0832c92fe4a7fb850b83ed7569b0f33</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T15:01:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-french-far-right-leader-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off--8f8e11bfae3f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-french-far-right-leader-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off" /><summary type="html">Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: 110.000 protesters in Paris</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e79b88a3edb1cd151f80536fa2bc2245b6493a63bf96f9110ec5ffbb8d5f1b26</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T15:00:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-110-000-protesters-in-paris--08d0963b371d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-110-000-protesters-in-paris" /><summary type="html">Hundreds of demonstrations took place across France on Saturday over the rising cost of living, with security forces deployed in large numbers to prevent a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. While the marches themselves “passed off well” in several cities, Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez said members of “ultra-left” groups threw projectiles and fireworks at police in an attempt to provoke clashes. French Communist Party spokesperson Fatima Khallouk shares more details.</summary></entry><entry><title>Calais, 10 years after the Jungle: Migrants still risk the Channel crossing</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ac6fab2c64afea38712a518a96c02307820a75329fcecef3d6fd890475dc8504</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T14:17:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-calais-10-years-after-the-jungle-migrants-still-risk-the-channel-crossing--d127e5ff3783.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-calais-10-years-after-the-jungle-migrants-still-risk-the-channel-crossing" /><summary type="html">Ten years ago, in 2016, thousands of #migrants lived in what was known as the “Great #Jungle”, a vast camp on the outskirts of #Calais. It took the authorities seven days to dismantle it.
 
Today, dozens of smaller “jungles” are scattered across a wider stretch of the coastline. These camps are dismantled every 48 hours on average.
 
For migrants, the goal remains the same: to cross the Channel and reach the United Kingdom. Despite the risks posed by “small boats”, these makeshift vessels, many continue to attempt the crossing in the hope of a better life.
 
Our reporters Pauline Godart, Claire Paccalin and Mohamed Farhat travelled to northern France for #BilletRetour.</summary></entry><entry><title>Presidential runoff under way in Brazil</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7815ed3eac55a4f1fdc83f07225c8020e4c3fadbf2cabff4056c945247409a36</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:46Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T13:52:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/presidential-runoff-under-way-in-brazil--bf9c7e32c5bd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/presidential-runoff-under-way-in-brazil" /><summary type="html">Brazil is also heading to the polls today for a run-off vote that will decide who leads the country. The race is on a knife edge between incumbent left-wing President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and right-wing Senator Flávio Bolsonaro. The son of former president Jair Bolsonaro, Flávio scored a major upset in the first round earlier this month, taking the lead in the vote. The campaign has been dominated by corruption scandals, economic difficulties and allegations of US interference, which Washington has denied. France 24 correspondent Jan Onoszko reports the latest from Rio de Janeiro.</summary></entry><entry><title>Le Pen leads polls with up to 38% six months before election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c3749d4c409b12bf5670cd8fc4b5a197e1b30a045670323b12b9146ae7cf174b</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:49Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T13:51:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/le-pen-leads-polls-with-up-to-38-six-months-before-election--ac3f248775c5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/le-pen-leads-polls-with-up-to-38-six-months-before-election" /><summary type="html">The day after the first day of the National Rally congress in Orléans, in central France, passed without any major incidents, Marine Le Pen is due to deliver her first major campaign speech on Sunday afternoon, 25 October, under heavy police surveillance. The speech, centred on the theme of renewal, aims to inject a dose of optimism six months ahead of the presidential election, the candidate has reportedly said in private.
 
Gilles Ivaldi, a political science researcher at the French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS) and the University of Nice, explains that the violence following protests across France in recent weeks could help the National Rally present itself as the party capable of restoring public safety and national security.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Le Pen launches presidential campaign in Orleans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cbb9264a12ee507bbcb4d59af3d49f0aaa8cc07260d2c54a73c71aec71dcac68</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:09:52Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T13:46:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-launches-presidential-campaign-in-orleans--72c4510f4dbf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-launches-presidential-campaign-in-orleans" /><summary type="html">Far-right leader Marine Le Pen is launching her presidential campaign today. The National Rally candidate is due to deliver her first campaign speech in Orléans, addressing around 10,000 party members. Police were controlling traffic entering the city this morning, following a large anti-far-right demonstration on Saturday, where thousands of protesters marched chanting anti-fascist slogans.</summary></entry><entry><title>The week in pictures: Protests across France, wildfires in Indonesia and Brazilian elections</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9e29d3d9d2a1fbf14872d79f3682e30db780078cd32c7de5314bd2865bbae94b</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T12:48:31Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/europe/20261025-the-week-in-pictures-protests-across-france-wildfires-in-indonesia-and-brazilian-elections--3fb12d327891.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261025-the-week-in-pictures-protests-across-france-wildfires-in-indonesia-and-brazilian-elections" /><summary type="html">From student protests across France over the weekend and presidential elections in Brazil pitting leftist incumbent Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva against right-wing challenger Flavio Bolsonaro to wildfires in Indonesia, FRANCE 24 looks back at some of the most striking images from the past week.</summary><category term="Europe" /></entry><entry><title>Voting under way across Brazil in tight presidential run-off between Lula and Flavio Bolsonaro</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7db069e6b45bd5105e782a282adb9b72b600a46b03346e427ec24664638a5ec8</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T12:46:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/americas/20261025-live-voting-under-way-across-brazil-in-tight-presidential-run-off-between-lula-and-bolsonar--05fa1f528dbc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261025-live-voting-under-way-across-brazil-in-tight-presidential-run-off-between-lula-and-bolsonaro" /><summary type="html">Brazil’s 158 million voters decide between leftist President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and right-wing challenger Senator Flavio Bolsonaro – the son of former president and Trump ally Jair Bolsonaro – following a highly charged run-off campaign period, with the candidates in a dead heat in polls. Follow our liveblog for the latest developments.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Polls open in Brazil presidential runoff</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7bb1bb9c80b2776710eea8a3478f8462e8868980b7abe7d89f1ed2a9b1306cfb</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:40:38Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/polls-open-in-brazil-presidential-runoff--8d55229e95d5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/polls-open-in-brazil-presidential-runoff" /><summary type="html">Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and sight-wing senator Flavio Bolsonaro faced off Friday in their second and final debate ahead of Sunday's presidential runoff election. The two candidates repeated their campaign proposals, focusing on the cost of living and security, while exchanging ⁠allegations of corruption. France 24 Jan Onoszko telles us the latest from Rio de Janeiro.</summary></entry><entry><title>Far-right leader Le Pen says visible policing keeps protests smooth</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:500ce6200226b3920c51258d4cf84ff222f52a72ee88e61ad502ac8c89674c9e</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:39:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/far-right-leader-le-pen-says-visible-policing-keeps-protests-smooth--be7e44d78d09.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/far-right-leader-le-pen-says-visible-policing-keeps-protests-smooth" /><summary type="html">Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan has been following the latest developments.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel: Demonstrators rally against government ahead of election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:236a50790d95387a6e0a88976a92a0961564a9a37654dc2a8aedf3ca6e5a67ae</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:38:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/israel-demonstrators-rally-against-government-ahead-of-election--605d00c22edc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-demonstrators-rally-against-government-ahead-of-election" /><summary type="html">In Israel, the anti-Netanyahu movement is mobilising. Thousands gathered in Tel Aviv on Friday to protest against the Israeli government, just three days ahead of a general election widely seen as a referendum on Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.</summary></entry><entry><title>Obama hits campaign trail for democrats</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:980ec20c47e9fc0409e10e5145c9f469d41483b55034717e1d3f7668fc213edc</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:36:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/obama-hits-campaign-trail-for-democrats--dc300583e461.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/obama-hits-campaign-trail-for-democrats" /><summary type="html">With less than two weeks to go before the US midterm elections, former President Barack Obama has hit the campaign trail, rallying support for Democratic candidates in the key states of Michigan and Ohio. He criticised the Trump administration, blaming the White House for rising living costs, a major concern for voters. Democrats are hoping to regain control of Congress, with polls showing several closely contested races. Ophelie Jacobson has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>West Bank: Families forcibly displaced in Hebron</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4bb36c5c979b8b025c741b9c30548d2300e8dcc53fc2fbb4d90d578f84f3dd60</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:35:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/west-bank-families-forcibly-displaced-in-hebron--c0b8d6b2a6c9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/west-bank-families-forcibly-displaced-in-hebron" /><summary type="html">More Palestinian families have been displaced in the occupied West Bank as Israeli forces continue operations in Hebron, following a deadly shooting that killed two Israeli soldiers on Friday. With elections scheduled for Tuesday, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has vowed a heavy response to the attack. Morgaene Ayre has the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>At least 1,300 people arrested in mass weekend protests across France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:50b1670d29b8a12ac6c2f217f2bd7e647a1a3a213250d2fc0d6d4d2c0045575c</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:02:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-at-least-1-300-people-arrested-in-mass-weekend-protests-across-france--e24f12ed1515.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-at-least-1-300-people-arrested-in-mass-weekend-protests-across-france" /><summary type="html">At least 1,300 people were arrested on the sidelines of Saturday's protests across France and at least 50 officers and three protesters were hurt, the interior ministry said Sunday. The arrests occurred as clashes broke out on the margins of largely calm demonstrations in major cities over the high cost of living.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>Suspect in racist Paris shooting transferred to psychiatric unit, had drugs in his system</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:69026add8ac76b7fe23c9ce13ffb4ddf73fbe8a573c9bb4077156a3cd732b90c</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T10:36:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-suspect-in-racist-gun-attack-near-paris-transferred-to-psychiatric-unit--4ab912be23c8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-suspect-in-racist-gun-attack-near-paris-transferred-to-psychiatric-unit" /><summary type="html">A shooter suspected of killing one person and wounding five others on Friday in the town of Trappes, near Paris, has had his police custody suspended for a 48-hour psychiatric observation, anti-terror prosecutors said Sunday. The 39-year-old man is believed to have had racist motivations behind the gun attack as most of the victims had foreign origins.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: Some clashes erupted at end of protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ecabf3beb093b0220e81b30bf394d4a3cd48b1afc8b850f89359d7f1a1a8269e</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:35:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-some-clashes-erupted-at-end-of-protests--0c66a7dc9b2a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-some-clashes-erupted-at-end-of-protests" /><summary type="html">There were some clashes on Saturday, mainly in major cities and as demonstrations came to an end. French authorities say police made nearly 1,000 arrests nationwide. Here’s a report from our sister channel, France 2.</summary></entry><entry><title>More than 400.000 protest across France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:69122fa2ae1bd05e83dc2958ff3bbabd863fffe16af89d594f7aa524e20f1367</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:32:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/more-than-400-000-protest-across-france--ddecf64571e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/more-than-400-000-protest-across-france" /><summary type="html">Saturday’s demonstrations across France were largely peaceful, easing fears of a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. Hundreds of thousands took to the streets to demand action on the rising cost of living and more funding for public services, with students, workers and retirees marching side by side. Many say their anger shows no sign of fading. The government put turnout at 410,000 nationwide, while the CGT union estimated the figure was almost twice as high. Here’s Jack Pownall with more.</summary></entry><entry><title>France’s far right gathers as Le Pen prepares her presidential campaign</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5dce4e85c7212ea93afdfd397dac1878c57bf4d214a6857e29bea213125e0dde</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:31:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-s-far-right-gathers-as-le-pen-prepares-her-presidential-campaign--73e7ad8469e1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-s-far-right-gathers-as-le-pen-prepares-her-presidential-campaign" /><summary type="html">Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. For more on what’s happening there, let’s cross to our reporter, Antonia Kerrigan.</summary></entry><entry><title>Hundreds of thousands march in France to protest against government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:68a8094777b4ec5e9633caf2c0199df9c38989fbeca4d64ffb434b0209827b6a</id><updated>2026-10-25T19:08:21Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:29:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/hundreds-of-thousands-march-in-france-to-protest-against-government--bc9fb991d30d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/hundreds-of-thousands-march-in-france-to-protest-against-government" /><summary type="html">Hundreds of thousands marched across France on Saturday protesting the rising cost of living and a lack of government resources for essential services like schools. While demonstrations in Paris started peacefully they ended with a heavy security response, with almost 1,000 arrested across the country. France 24 International Affairs Editor Oliver Farry shares further details.</summary></entry></feed>
